      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "A multiple of 9 is divisible by each of its digits, since\n$9 = 3 \\times 3$. For a number to contain this property, it is\ntherefore only necessary that the digits come from a set of numbers whose sum is a factor of 9. The number 10, for instance, comes from the set $\\{0,1\\}$ and therefore is not divisible by the sum of its digits. Hence there are no lucky integers that are also multiples of 9. Since there are multiples of 9 that are not lucky integers, the least multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer must be one more than the least lucky integer $n$ that is a multiple of 9, which is 18. $\\boxed{19}$\nAlternatively, if you skipped to this part, consider that one more than the least unlucky integer, 19, is itself a lucky integer as well as a multiple of 9. Hence, the least multiple of 9 that is unlucky must be 19.",
