    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the least positive multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer, we must analyze numbers that are divisible by 9 and determine which are lucky.\n\nCounting the digits of a number, we add them up and divide by 9, and if the quotient is an integer, add that quotient to a number if it's greater than 0. We then check if the incremented number is divisible by the original sum of digits.\n\nFor the number $9k$, if $k > 9$, then incrementing $k$ (e.g., by $1...9$) will result in $9$ being a factor of $9k$. \nFor instance:\n\\begin{align*}\n9+6 &> 0 &&\\implies 15 &&{} \\\\\n9+96 &> 0 &&\\implies 105 &&{} \\\\\n9+996 &> 0 &&\\implies 1095 &&{} \\\\\n9+9996 &> 0 &&\\implies 10995 &&{} \\\\\n9+99996 &> 0 &&\\implies 109995 &&{} \\\\\n9+999996 &> 0 &&\\implies 1099995 &&{} \\end{align*}\nIf $k = 9k + 1 99 ... 9$, the incremented number is $9k + 109...99 + 9$ ($9k$ plus the units digit of the original divisor, plus 9 multiplies by 9 from the other units for each carry which amounts to $9k + 90$).\n\nHence the least positive multiple of 9 that is not lucky is $\\boxed{9900}$.\n\nThe answer is: 9900",
